PASS level_intents: all 120 levels have a specific intent
PASS no_filler_levels: no trivial filler levels after the tutorial
FAIL level_design: level-design review 7.3/10 (bar 8), 0 level(s) below 6/10: A polished, well-paced 120-level set with a clear one-idea-per-island spine and strong picture variety. Casual/skilled win rates land on the studio targets and trap rate is 0 everywhere. The main faults are repetition of the "exact row plus full column" opener, teach levels for cracked and deep-chain that are harder than they should be, and several breathers and combines that do not live up to their labels. About 40% of levels are solid-but-forgettable (6-7), and roughly 45% are genuinely good (8+). There are no broken levels. Curve: Strong chapter-per-idea structure. Each island adds one rule (reach, colour, void, stamp, colour-cross, smudged, cracked, deep chain, then mixes). Chapter order is sensible: teach, teach, practise, practise, twist, boss, breather, twist, practise, combine, twist, finale. Easy-run and difficulty targets are met. Ch.1 L1-3 are 100/100/93%, the last third averages about 62% casual, and there are no isolated spikes.

Weaknesses:
1. Many levels open the same way: one exact-fit row plus one full column, then a chain. The intent text is repetitive, and "aha" variety falls off in the middle chapters (Pigment Market, Conservatory, Observatory).
2. Several "teach" levels are harder than the neighbouring practise levels. The Kiln cracked teaches are 71/69%, the Observatory chain teaches 70/70%, and L85 follows the 63% L84. A teach level should sit at about 85%+ casual.
3. Some "breathers" are not light. L55 has 7 passes and a stamp-only entry, L103 is 83%, and L81-style levels run at 64%. A breather should be 90% or higher and take 3-4 passes.
4. "Combine" and "twist" levels sometimes use the mechanic incidentally (L40, L45, L109). The new rule should be the aha, not decoration.
5. Chapters 7-10 jump straight from teach to 55-65% casual with no gentle plateau. This is on target overall, but it is steep within Kiln Room and Lantern Gallery.
6. There is no recap of the earlier mechanics (void, stamp) in the later chapters, apart from L70, L72, L110, L113 and L120. That is good, but sparse.
7. Several intent texts contain loose claims. L63 gives the column 6 description loosely, and L80 and L104 name lines whose clues are not self-evident.
8. Three picture silhouettes are near duplicates in structure: L33 and L36 awnings, L26 and L28 contrast levels.
REDESIGN THESE LEVELS (keep the ones not listed):
L19 (6/10): 92% casual and a repeat of L7's row-plus-column stem shape. Forgettable. → Use a different breather shape, such as a symmetric picture with one exact row, and drop the stem.
L28 (6/10): Repeats the L26 rule with no new idea. → Use the rind as a cross-check where the colour edge is read from the columns.
L33 (6/10): Near-symmetric stripes with 3 passes are easy for a combine. Reads as busywork. → Break symmetry on one stripe and add a partial row.
L35 (6/10): Repeats the row-5 exact-fit-with-touch pattern of L29 and L33. → Replace the rim row with a column-first anchor.
L40 (6/10): The voids are incidental; the level is solved by full rows and columns. → Make a void cut a run that decides a column.
L81 (6/10): 64% casual for a mid-combine with a lot of dense fill; solving leans on hidden-cell guessing risk. → Give one extra stamped cell in column 3.
L3 (7/10): Tiny 5x6 with a loose silhouette; the jellyfish is hard to read. → Add a tentacle row or widen to 6x7 so the overlap aha reads as a picture.
L7 (7/10): Fine, but it re-teaches L2's exact-fit idea with no new angle. → Make the bucket a split-clue row that only fits via one gap-free hint.
L10 (7/10): Standard combo; no standout insight beyond L4. → Make the X-pinning of the 9 and the 8 the single deduction the player must find.
L15 (7/10): Reads close to L13's idea; the owl is a bit blobby. → Lean on the lone-1 branch aha and reduce the solid body mass.
L16 (7/10): Near repeat of L15's perch logic. → Swap the premise so the lone 1 is not on a perch.
L17 (7/10): Large 11x11 with a lot of solid body, so the work is mechanical. → Open the body with a couple of paper holes so columns need reach logic.
L21 (7/10): Fine, but column 10 opening it is the same trick as earlier chapters. → Start from a different anchor, a split clue rather than 1 8.
L25 (7/10): Row 3 fills outright, so there is little to figure out. The teach is clear but the aha is thin. → Add a second row where touch versus gap changes a placement.
L27 (7/10): Colour order alone decides it, but only one row shows that. → Add a second row decided by colour order.
L29 (7/10): Good rule, thin picture. → Add a second line using the same rule.
L31 (7/10): 93% casual and a fine breather, but 20 lines open at the start is a lot. → Fine as is; trim 2-3 open lines.
L32 (7/10): Mechanical. → Use the middle-1 stem aha on a second line.
L39 (7/10): Large and fiddly; the face is hard to read. → Trim the grid or open the face with one more landmark.
L42 (7/10): 73% casual is fine, but 9s with ten cells is the same trick as L8. → Add a void-cut column that decides the plinth.
L43 (7/10): Not much easier than neighbours at 87%. → Simplify the lid so the voids do more of the work.
L44 (7/10): Fingers are long and a little repetitive. → Vary the finger gaps further.
L47 (7/10): Long explanation; the aha is spread across several lines. → Pick one decisive line and trim the rest.
L49 (7/10): Fine, but stamps are single-cell and easy to miss. → Add a tutorial highlight for the stamp.
L51 (7/10): Same lesson as L50. → Use a stamp in the middle of a run instead of a lone 1.

